= Solution
{parent=i}

Use the <ratio-of-uniforms method> on
$$
\mathcal R=\{(u,v):0<u\leq1,\ u^2\leq h(v/u)\}.
$$
Writing $x=v/u$ gives $u\leq e^{-x^2/(4\sigma^2)}$ and $v=ux$. Consequently
$$
|v|\leq\max_{x\geq0}x e^{-x^2/(4\sigma^2)}
=\sigma\sqrt{2/e},
$$
because the derivative vanishes at $x=\sigma\sqrt2$. Thus the <normal ratio-of-uniforms envelope> is the rectangle $0<u<1$, $|v|<\sigma\sqrt{2/e}$.

Generate <independent> $U_1,U_2$ with <uniform distribution> on $(0,1)$, set $u=U_1$ and $v=(2U_2-1)\sigma\sqrt{2/e}$, and accept precisely when
$$
u^2\leq\exp\!\left[-\frac{(v/u)^2}{2\sigma^2}\right].
$$
Reject and repeat otherwise. **On acceptance return $X=v/u$.** One can compare logarithms instead of exponentials to avoid underflow.

For a proof, the change of variables $(u,x)\mapsto(u,v=ux)$ has <Jacobian determinant> $u$. A uniform point in $\mathcal R$ therefore gives an $x$-density proportional to
$$
\int_0^{\sqrt{h(x)}}u\,du=\frac{h(x)}2.
$$
Normalizing yields the required <normal distribution> with <mean> zero and <variance> $\sigma^2$. The region area is $\frac12\int h(x)\,dx=\sigma\sqrt{\pi/2}$; dividing it by the rectangle area $2\sigma\sqrt{2/e}$ gives acceptance <probability> $\sqrt{\pi e}/4$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $\alpha,\beta>0$, integrating the <Weibull distribution> <probability density function> gives
$$
G(x)=1-e^{-x^\alpha/\beta}\quad(x\geq0).
$$
The <inverse transform sampling> method takes $U$ uniform on $(0,1)$ and solves $G(X)=U$. Hence
$$
\boxed{X=\{-\beta\log(1-U)\}^{1/\alpha}.}
$$
Since $1-U$ is also uniform, $\{-\beta\log U\}^{1/\alpha}$ is an equivalent implementation. For any $x\geq0$, the resulting event $X\leq x$ has <probability> $G(x)$, verifying its distribution.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Assume $0\leq p\leq1$. Draw an <independent> component indicator with a <Bernoulli distribution> of success <probability> $p$. On success, generate the <normal distribution> from part (i) and return its absolute value; this has the <half-normal distribution> <probability density function> $2f(x)$ on $x\geq0$. On failure, generate the <Weibull distribution> from part (ii). Use fresh <independent> uniforms for the chosen component.

The law of total <probability> gives the output <probability density function>
$$
\boxed{p\,2f(x)+(1-p)g(x)\quad(x\geq0),}
$$
as required. The factor two belongs inside the half-normal component; the <probability> of choosing that component is $p$, not $2p$.

= 4
{parent=Paper 47}
