= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For <independent> draws $X_i$ from a normalized proposal $g$, use the <importance sampling> identity
$$
\mu=\int\theta(x)f(x)\,dx
=\mathbb E_g\!\left[\theta(X)\frac{f(X)}{g(X)}\right].
$$
Thus the <unbiased estimator> and its <variance> are
$$
\boxed{\widehat\mu=\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n\theta(X_i)\frac{f(X_i)}{g(X_i)},\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(\widehat\mu)=\frac1n\left[\int\frac{\theta(x)^2f(x)^2}{g(x)}\,dx-\mu^2\right].}
$$
The <probability support> condition is $g>0$ wherever $\theta f\ne0$, and the <variance> formula requires a finite <second moment>. Since both <probability densities> are normalized, no self-normalization of weights is needed.

In the proposed example, normalize $g$ to $g(x)=3x^2$ on $(0,1)$. It is sampled by $X=U^{1/3}$ for uniform $U$. Taking $\theta(x)=x$ gives
$$
\boxed{\widehat\mu_g=\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n\frac4{3\pi}\frac{\sqrt{1-X_i^2}}{X_i},
\qquad X_i\sim3x^2\mathbf1_{(0,1)}(x).}
$$
As a check, $\mu_g=\frac4\pi\int_0^1x\sqrt{1-x^2}\,dx=4/(3\pi)$. The <second moment> of one weighted observation is
$$
\int_0^1\frac{x^2f(x)^2}{3x^2}\,dx
=\frac{16}{3\pi^2}\int_0^1(1-x^2)\,dx
=\frac{32}{9\pi^2},
$$
so $\operatorname{Var}(\widehat\mu_g)=16/(9\pi^2n)$. The weight's singularity at zero does not make this <variance> infinite.

To prove the optimal proposal rule, apply the <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality>:
$$
\left(\int|\theta(x)|f(x)\,dx\right)^2
=\left(\int\frac{|\theta(x)|f(x)}{\sqrt{g(x)}}\sqrt{g(x)}\,dx\right)^2
\leq\int\frac{\theta(x)^2f(x)^2}{g(x)}\,dx.
$$
Equality holds when the two factors are proportional almost everywhere, giving the <minimum-variance importance distribution>
$$
\boxed{g_0(x)=\frac{|\theta(x)|f(x)}{\int|\theta(u)|f(u)\,du}.}
$$
For the present nonnegative integrand, normalization gives
$$
\boxed{g_0(x)=3x\sqrt{1-x^2}\quad(0<x<1).}
$$
Under this proposal, $xf(x)/g_0(x)=4/(3\pi)$ is constant, so the <variance> is exactly zero. If sampling is wanted, its distribution function is $1-(1-x^2)^{3/2}$, with inverse $X=\sqrt{1-(1-U)^{2/3}}$. In general the optimal proposal's <normalizing constant> can itself be the unknown integral, limiting this zero-variance construction's practical use.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The plug-in estimate is
$$
\boxed{\widehat\theta=\widehat\beta_1\widehat\beta_2.}
$$
For a concrete <bootstrap> construction, suppose the rows $(x_i,y_i)$ are <independent and identically distributed>, $\mathbb E(e_i\mid x_i)=0$, the design moment matrix is nonsingular, and the required moments exist. Use a <paired bootstrap>: sample $n$ rows with replacement, form $X^*,Y^*$, refit by <ordinary least squares>, and compute $\widehat\theta^*=\widehat\beta_1^*\widehat\beta_2^*$. Repeat many times. Singular resampled designs require a suitable rank-preserving implementation or replacement replicate; with a regular continuous design their frequency vanishes asymptotically.

The <bootstrap-t confidence interval> method also needs a <standard error> in the original sample and every replicate. One asymptotically valid heteroscedasticity-robust <covariance> estimate is
$$
\widehat V=(X^\top X)^{-1}\left(\sum_i x_ix_i^\top\widehat e_i^2\right)(X^\top X)^{-1}.
$$
The <delta method> for the coefficient product gives
$$
d=(\widehat\beta_2,\widehat\beta_1)^\top,\qquad
\boxed{\widehat s=(d^\top\widehat Vd)^{1/2}.}
$$
Recompute $\widehat V^*,d^*,\widehat s^*$ in each paired replicate. This is <bootstrap inference for a product of regression coefficients>.

With fixed rather than random design, resample centered residuals if errors are identically distributed with <homoskedasticity>, or use a properly specified <wild bootstrap> for heteroscedastic errors, keeping $X$ fixed and refitting after generating $Y^*$. The printed assumption $\mathbb E e_i=0$ alone does not establish <independent> observations or validity of a <bootstrap confidence interval>. The formulas below give asymptotic 95% coverage under the stated regularity conditions, not an exact finite-sample guarantee. In particular the ordinary first-order studentization needs $(\beta_1,\beta_2)\ne(0,0)$ and a nonzero asymptotic <variance>; when both coefficients vanish the product has nonstandard behavior.

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $q^*_{.025}$ and $q^*_{.975}$ be the empirical <quantiles> of the paired-bootstrap values $\widehat\theta^*$. The <percentile bootstrap confidence interval> is
$$
\boxed{[q^*_{.025},q^*_{.975}].}
$$
Its endpoints use the <bootstrap> distribution of the estimate itself. The regularity assumptions and resampling scheme are those in the parent solution.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For each <bootstrap> replicate form the studentized pivot
$$
T^*=\frac{\widehat\theta^*-\widehat\theta}{\widehat s^*}.
$$
Let $t^*_{.025},t^*_{.975}$ be its empirical <quantiles>. The <bootstrap-t confidence interval> approximates
$$
\mathbb P\!\left(t^*_{.025}\leq\frac{\widehat\theta-\theta}{\widehat s}
\leq t^*_{.975}\right)\simeq0.95.
$$
Solving both inequalities for $\theta$ reverses the <quantile> order, giving
$$
\boxed{[\widehat\theta-t^*_{.975}\widehat s,\quad
\widehat\theta-t^*_{.025}\widehat s].}
$$
Using the same fixed <standard error> in every <bootstrap> numerator would not implement this studentized procedure; each replicate needs its own $\widehat s^*$.

